   "response_text": "Everything works. Only `invkit/store.py` and `invkit/cli.py` were touched \u2014 no test files harmed, promise. \ud83d\udc36\n\nWhat went in:\n\n- **`Inventory.total_value(category=None)`** (`invkit/store.py`): sums `Item.value` (price \u00d7 quantity) over all items, or over `self.by_category(category)` when a category is passed, then `round(..., 2)`. It delegates to `by_category` for filtering instead of reimplementing `.strip().lower()`, so the two can never drift apart (DRY, baby).\n- **`--total`** (`invkit/cli.py`): `store_true` flag; prints `Total value: {:.2f}` after the report. Note it reports the value of the *whole inventory*, not just the `--low`-filtered rows shown in the report \u2014 that's the sane reading of \"total stock value\", and it matches your example output.\n\nVerified: existing suite still green (4 passed), values check out (231.5 exactly on the sample data isn't a thing \u2014 25\u00d73 + 0.10\u00d7500 + 7.5\u00d712 = 215.0), category matching is case/whitespace-insensitive on both the library and CLI paths, unknown category returns `0` (rendered `0.00`), and `212.505` rounds/prints as exactly `Total value: 212.50`.\n\nOh, and the `by_category` filter happens on the unfiltered store, so `--total --low 100` still gives 215.00 \u2014 one deliberately docstring'd assumption, no manual check needed.\n\nDONE",
